window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["jl"]=[{"id":"jl-dana44-jl-front","title":"Dana 44 AdvanTEK Front \u2014 Service and Upgrade","vehicle":"jl","category":"axles","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":1800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","13mm socket","fluid pump","torque wrench","RTV","drain pan"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","diff cover service"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2025"],"tags":["axles","drivetrain","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar 75W-140 Synthetic Gear Oil (qt)","part_number":"68218655AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Mopar Front Diff Cover Gasket / RTV Kit","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Spicer Chromoly Front Shafts (FAD-delete, M210)","part_number":"10044469","vendor":"Spicer","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"Next Venture Motorsports M210 Front Axle Truss","vendor":"Next Venture Motorsports","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Artec Industries M210 Differential Cover","vendor":"Artec","price_estimate_usd":220}],"citations":[{"title":"Spicer JL M210/Dana 44 AdvanTEK Upgrades","url":"https://danaproparts.com/p-35861-jeep-wrangler-jl-dana-spicer-parts-upgrades.html"},{"title":"JL Wrangler Maintenance Schedule","url":"https://getgaragehub.com/guides/jeep-wrangler-jl-maintenance-schedule/"},{"title":"JL Front Axle Truss \u2014 AdvanTEK / M210","url":"https://nextventuremotorsports.com/products/jl-jt-front-axle-truss-advantek-dana-44-m210"},{"title":"Jeep Wrangler Differential Fluid Capacity Chart","url":"https://motobilt.com/blogs/tech/jeep-wrangler-differential-fluid-capacity-chart"}],"alternatives":["jl-differential-fluid-jl","jl-dana44-jl-rear","jl-rubicon-jl-front"],"summary":"Service the JL front Dana 44 AdvanTEK every 30,000 miles with 2 quarts of 75W-140 \u2014 and know that the FAD housing and stock axle shafts are the two weak points that snap first when you go bigger than 35s.","description":"The JL front axle is a Dana 44 AdvanTEK on Rubicon trims (model M210 wide-track) and an M210 Dana 44-derived front on Sahara/Sport with smaller ring gear. Both share the same housing platform and the same Front Axle Disconnect (FAD) on non-Rubicon trims. The Rubicon's electronic locker lives in this housing. Service fluid is Mopar 75W-140 synthetic GL-5, capacity about 2.0 quarts at the bottom of the fill hole. Change interval is every 30,000 miles under normal driving and every 15,000 miles for vehicles that see water crossings, sand, or regular trail use.\n\nThe FAD casting on Sport and Sahara trims is the part to watch. The cast aluminum FAD housing is brittle and is the most common failure point on stock JL fronts, and the plastic FAD coupler bushing inside it wears out and dumps gear oil. If your build is heading past 35-inch tires, the honest move is a FAD-delete with Spicer chromoly inner shafts (PN 10044469 set) \u2014 this swaps the plastic coupler for a solid one-piece shaft and gets rid of the disconnect mechanism entirely. Rubicons skip this issue because they came with the locking version and no FAD.\n\nStock M210 axle shafts are adequate up to 35-inch tires for trail use. Past 35s, especially with lockers and heavy throttle, expect to bend or twist stock shafts within a season or two of hard wheeling. Chromoly upgrades push the working limit to 37-inch tires with the rest of the drivetrain unchanged. An axle truss (Next Venture Motorsports or Artec) adds housing rigidity for builds running 37s+ with lockers \u2014 necessary on rigs that see rock crawling, optional for trail rigs.\n\nBall joints on M210s are a known wear item. Listen for clunks at full lock and inspect for grease weeping at every fluid change. Replace as a set with Synergy or Dynatrac ball joints; the stock joints are unsealed in the lower position and gravel finds its way in.\n\nAftermarket diff covers (Artec, ARB, Rusty's, AEV) add about 4 ounces of capacity and a magnetic drain plug. They protect the housing on rocks and make fluid changes cleaner because of the drain plug \u2014 the stock cover requires you to pull the whole cover to drain.","steps":["Park on level ground, warm the axle by driving 5\u201310 miles so the old fluid drains cleanly.","Position drain pan under the diff cover. Crack the fill plug (top of cover, 3/8\" square drive) FIRST to confirm it backs out \u2014 if it's stuck and you've already drained, you can't refill.","If you have an aftermarket cover with a drain plug, pull the plug and drain. Otherwise, loosen the cover bolts in a crisscross pattern, leave the top two snug, and pry the bottom of the cover loose to drain.","Remove the cover, wipe the housing clean, inspect the ring gear teeth for pitting or chipping, and check the magnet for metal chunks (fine paste is normal; chunks are not).","Scrape old RTV off both mating surfaces with a plastic scraper. Wipe with brake cleaner.","Apply 1/4\" bead of Mopar gray RTV or equivalent silicone around the cover, going inside the bolt holes. Install cover and torque bolts to 25 ft-lbs in a crisscross pattern.","Wait 30 minutes for RTV to set, then fill through the fill hole until fluid is at the bottom of the threads (about 2 quarts). Reinstall fill plug to 30 ft-lbs.","Drive 20\u201350 miles and recheck for leaks at the cover and fill plug."],"pros":["Fluid change is a one-hour job with a $40 parts bill","Service catches FAD wear, ball joint slop, and locker-fluid contamination","Chromoly shaft upgrade is a bolt-in once the axle is open"],"cons":["FAD housing is a known weak point on non-Rubicon trims","Stock shafts bend on 37s+ \u2014 plan a chromoly budget if you're going big","Mopar 75W-140 is not cheap and the spec is firm \u2014 generic 80W-90 will damage the electric locker on Rubicons"]},{"id":"jl-dana44-jl-rear","title":"Dana 44 AdvanTEK Rear \u2014 Locker Options","vehicle":"jl","category":"axles","subcategory":"lockers","type":"part","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":700,"high":1700},"tools_required":["dial indicator","bearing press","torque wrench","case spreader (optional)","shop manual"],"skills_required":["gear setup","pinion preload","backlash measurement"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["advantek","m220","m210","locker","rubicon"],"parts":[{"name":"Eaton ELocker \u2014 Dana 44 M220 35-spline (Non-Rubicon JL/JT)","part_number":"14234-1","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"Eaton ELocker \u2014 Dana 44 M220 35-spline (alt p/n)","part_number":"14313-2","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":1150},{"name":"Eaton Performance ELocker \u2014 30-spline D44, 3.73 and lower","part_number":"19977-010","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":900},{"name":"ARB Air Locker RD-245 \u2014 Dana 44 30-spline w/factory CV","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":1091},{"name":"ARB compact onboard compressor (CKMA12)","part_number":"CKMA12","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":340}],"citations":[{"title":"Eaton 14234-1 Dana 44 Electric Locker \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/eaton/dana-44-electric-locker-wrangler-jl-gladiator-jt-14234-1"},{"title":"Eaton 14313-2 35 Spline D44/M220 E-Locker \u2014 OffRoad Elements","url":"https://www.offroadelements.com/eaton-e-locker-35-spline-d44-m220-wrangler-jl-gladiator-jt-2018/"},{"title":"Eaton 19977-010 4-Pinion ELocker 30-spline D44 \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/eaton/performance-4-pinion-e-locker-30-spline-dana-44-19977-010"}],"alternatives":["jl-dana44-jl-front","jl-rubicon-jl-front"],"summary":"Non-Rubicon JLs have an open Dana 44 M220 rear axle that benefits enormously from a locker; Eaton's 14234-1 ELocker is the direct-fit electric answer ($1,100), ARB's RD-245 is the air-actuated alternative if you already run a compressor.","description":"The JL rear axle is a Dana 44 in name only \u2014 the housing is the M220 variant, beefier than the front M210 but with its own carrier specs and spline counts. Rubicons come with the OE Tru-Lok electric locker from the factory. Sport, Sport S, Sahara, and Willys come with an open differential (Sahara is sometimes Trac-Lok limited-slip) and a 30- or 32-spline carrier depending on year and gear ratio. Knowing exactly what you have determines which locker fits.\n\nEaton's ELocker is the most common upgrade because it's electric, one-wire activation, and doesn't require an air compressor. The 14234-1 fits 35-spline M220 carriers on 2018\u20132023 JL/JT non-Rubicons. The newer 14313-2 covers later model years through 2026. The 19977-010 is the performance 4-pinion option for older 30-spline D44s with 3.73 or numerically lower gears \u2014 relevant only if you're running an older D44 conversion or stock gears. Get the spline count from your build sheet or, more reliably, by pulling the diff cover and counting splines on the side gears.\n\nARB Air Lockers are the air-actuated alternative. The RD-245 fits 30-spline factory-CV Dana 44s and costs $1,091 plus another $340 for the compressor (CKMA12) if you don't have one. Two locker reasons to choose ARB: you want full-open behavior on the street (ELockers are also open until engaged, so this is a wash), and you want the option to add a front ARB later sharing the same compressor. If you're already running OBA for tires, the ARB compressor is a no-cost adder.\n\nInstall is not a beginner job. You're pulling the carrier, swapping in the locker, setting pinion preload, ring gear backlash (0.005\u20130.008\"), and pinion bearing crush (~15 in-lb rolling torque used). If you're not regearing at the same time, you can re-use the existing ring gear if it's not worn \u2014 but most shops will tell you to regear during the locker install since the carrier is out anyway. If this is your first gear setup, pay a driveline shop. Bad setup eats a $1,100 locker.","steps":["Confirm axle: M220 vs older D44, spline count (30/32/35), and current gear ratio (stamped on ring gear or check VIN build sheet).","Drain rear diff fluid. Pull the cover.","Mark and remove the carrier bearing caps. Note shim locations and orientation.","Pry the carrier out. Measure ring-and-pinion backlash before removing anything (note for re-setup).","Press old carrier bearings, install on new locker carrier with same shims to start.","Drop new locker into housing, install bearing caps, torque to spec (55-70 ft-lbs typical).","Re-check backlash (0.005\u20130.008\") and pattern. Adjust shims as needed.","Run actuator wire (ELocker) along axle tube to a frame-mounted switch, or run ARB air line.","Fill diff with 75W-85 or 75W-90 gear oil \u2014 Rubicon trac-lok pumpkins also need friction modifier; locker pumpkins do not.","Bench-test actuator before reinstalling driveshaft. Verify locker engages and disengages cleanly."],"pros":["Eaton ELocker is fully open until engaged \u2014 no street manners trade-off","One-wire install, no air system needed for ELocker","ARB shares compressor with front locker if planning both","Massive traction gain on a non-Rubicon JL"],"cons":["$1,100+ for the locker alone, more if regearing","Setup is unforgiving \u2014 backlash off by 0.002\" eats a ring gear","Need to know your spline count before ordering \u2014 wrong locker = restock fee","ARB requires a compressor, more plumbing"]},{"id":"rubicon-jl-front","title":"JL Rubicon Dana 44 \u2014 What Transfers Across","vehicle":"jl","category":"axles","subcategory":"front_axle","type":"swap","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":1800,"high":4500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","torque wrench","spring compressor","pickle fork"],"skills_required":["axle swap experience","brake bleeding","ABS reset"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2026"],"tags":["dana-44","m210","front-axle","rubicon-swap","locker"],"parts":[{"name":"JL Rubicon M210 front axle assembly (used)","vendor":"Quadratec or salvage","price_estimate_usd":2200},{"name":"Rubicon front driveshaft (used)","vendor":"salvage","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"AEV ProCal Snap programmer","part_number":"30401050AB","vendor":"AEV","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Front track bar (heavy duty, if re-using stock arms)","vendor":"Synergy","price_estimate_usd":280}],"citations":[{"title":"Identifying Wranglers and Their Different Axles \u2014 Revolution Gear & Axle","url":"https://revolutiongear.com/blog/identifying-wranglers-and-their-different-axles/"},{"title":"Which Jeep Wrangler Axles Do I Have? \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/blog/which-jeep-wrangler-axles-do-i-have"}],"alternatives":["dana-44-swap-aftermarket","locker-only-upgrade"],"summary":"A JL Rubicon front Dana 44 (M210) bolts into a Sport or Sahara JL with the right gear ratio match, but it's 1.5\" wider per side, comes with a 4.10 final drive, and requires a recalibration before it'll behave on the road.","description":"The JL Rubicon front axle is technically a Dana M210HD \u2014 Dana's modern designation for the wide-knuckle, electric-locker variant Jeep ships under Rubicon trims. Non-Rubicon JLs get the narrower M186 (often called Dana 30 internally). The two share suspension mounting points and brake hardware, which is what makes the swap appealing \u2014 bolt-pattern compatibility means you're not fabricating brackets.\n\nWhat does NOT transfer cleanly is the track width and gearing. The Rubicon axle sits 1.5\" wider per wheel flange, which pushes the front tires outboard and changes scrub radius. Stock fender flares cover it, but expect rub on full lock. More importantly, Rubicons ship with 4.10 gears; Sports and Saharas typically run 3.45 or 3.73. If the rear axle doesn't get re-geared to match, the transfer case binds and the driveshafts wind up against each other on dry pavement. Either re-gear the rear or plan on swapping both axles as a pair.\n\nThe electric locker is the real prize. It engages through the Rubicon-only switch panel, which non-Rubicon JLs don't have. Wiring the locker requires either a salvaged Rubicon dash harness or a standalone controller (sPOD, Switch Pros). AEV's ProCal Snap will tell the BCM about the new axle, gear ratio, and locker so the dashboard and ABS behave normally \u2014 without it, expect persistent dash warnings and incorrect speedometer readings.\n\nDriveshaft length is the other gotcha. Rubicon front driveshafts are slightly shorter to match the wider axle pinion offset. Trying to re-use a Sport driveshaft will cause vibration and premature U-joint wear. Source the matching Rubicon driveshaft from the same donor.\n\nCross-generation note: JL Rubicon axles are NOT interchangeable with JK Rubicon axles. The JL uses a redesigned knuckle, different ball joints, and an updated electronic locker controller. Don't try to mix generations.","steps":["Source a complete JL Rubicon front axle assembly with knuckles, hubs, calipers, ABS sensors, and pinion yoke intact.","Confirm gear ratio on the donor matches your rear axle (or plan to re-gear the rear).","Support the JL on jack stands, remove front tires, disconnect ABS, brake lines, sway bar links, and track bar.","Unbolt control arms at the axle and lower the stock axle out.","Position the Rubicon axle, reattach control arms, track bar, and sway bar links.","Reconnect brake lines (use new banjo washers), bleed the system.","Reconnect ABS sensors and the electric locker harness \u2014 splice into a Rubicon donor harness or wire to an aftermarket controller.","Use AEV ProCal Snap to write the new axle code and gear ratio to the BCM.","Test on a flat lot in 2WD before hitting trails. Verify ABS, locker engagement, and steering centering."],"pros":["Factory locker with proper integration","Wider stance improves stability","Stronger axle shafts and ring gear than M186","Mostly bolt-in if donor is complete"],"cons":["Locker wiring is the hard part \u2014 plan it before you start","Wider track means new wheels or spacers off the table without rub","Must match rear axle gearing or re-gear both","AEV ProCal required for proper BCM integration"]},{"id":"jl-brake-pads-jl","title":"Front and Rear Brake Pad Replacement","vehicle":"jl","category":"brakes","subcategory":"pads_rotors","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":280},"tools_required":["19mm socket for lug nuts","13mm and 15mm sockets for caliper bracket","C-clamp or piston compressor","brake cleaner","torque wrench","anti-seize for sliding pins","high-temp brake grease"],"skills_required":["brake-system competence","torque awareness"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-present"],"tags":["brakes","pads","rotors","safety-critical","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Akebono ACT2018 ceramic front pads","part_number":"ACT2018","vendor":"Akebono","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Akebono ACT2019 ceramic rear pads","part_number":"ACT2019","vendor":"Akebono","price_estimate_usd":50},{"name":"Mopar OE front pads (Rubicon spec)","part_number":"68249847AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Centric Premium front rotors (pair)","part_number":"120.67098","vendor":"Centric","price_estimate_usd":110}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar JL Wrangler brake torque specs \u2014 caliper bracket 110 ft-lb, slide pins 26 ft-lb","url":"https://www.moparrepairconnect.com"},{"title":"JL Wrangler Forums: brake pad and rotor part numbers thread","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Front pads and rotors take 90 minutes; rears another 60. Caliper bracket bolts are 110 ft-lb, slide pins are 26 ft-lb, lugs are 130 ft-lb. Don't reuse worn slide pin boots \u2014 torn boots cause uneven pad wear within 5,000 miles.","description":"JL Wrangler brakes are larger than JK across the lineup \u2014 the front rotor went from 12.9\" on JK Sport to 13.0\" Sport/13.4\" Rubicon on JL. The system is otherwise conventional floating caliper with single-piston front and rear. Pad and rotor service is straightforward once you understand the bracket vs slide pin distinction.\n\n**Pad choice.** OE Mopar pads are quiet but dust-heavy. Akebono ACT-series ceramic pads stay clean and bite well below 1,000\u00b0F \u2014 the right choice for daily-drivers and mild trail use. Hawk LTS gives more cold bite for tow rigs. Avoid no-name pads from auction sites; they'll glaze and squeal within 6 months.\n\n**Rotor decision.** Rotors are sacrificial. If yours have <0.030\" of useful thickness left (verify with a micrometer against the minimum stamped on the rotor hat), replace them. Centric Premium and Bosch QuietCast are both quiet and last 50K+ miles. Slotted/drilled rotors look aggressive but offer no benefit for the JL's weight class and crack faster.\n\n**Slide pins matter more than pads.** A torn slide pin boot lets sand and water into the pin bore. The caliper then sticks on one side, the inboard pad wears faster than outboard, and you replace pads at half life. Inspect both pins every brake service. Re-grease with high-temp synthetic brake grease \u2014 never wheel bearing grease (it melts).","steps":["Loosen lug nuts before lifting. Lift one wheel, support on stand. Remove wheel.","Inspect the caliper for leaks at the dust boot and at the hose fitting. A wet caliper goes in the rebuild pile, not the reinstall pile.","Open the master cylinder cap to let air escape as the pistons retract. (Don't let the reservoir overflow.)","Pull the two 13mm slide pin bolts on the caliper. Lift caliper off the bracket. Hang it from the spring with a wire \u2014 never let it hang from the brake hose.","Slide the old pads out of the bracket. Note the wear pattern \u2014 uneven wear = stuck slide pin.","Pull the slide pins out of the bracket. Wipe clean. Check the boots \u2014 replace if torn. Re-grease with high-temp brake grease and reseat.","(If replacing rotors) Pull the two 15mm bracket bolts and lift bracket off. Remove the rotor (may need a few hammer taps if rust-stuck). Wipe new rotor clean with brake cleaner \u2014 they ship with rust preventative oil. Reinstall bracket, 110 ft-lb on bolts.","Insert new pads into the bracket. Apply a thin smear of brake grease on the pad backing where it contacts the caliper piston and the slide ears.","Compress the caliper piston with a C-clamp using an old pad as the contact surface. Push slowly so the master doesn't overflow.","Reinstall caliper over new pads. Torque the 13mm slide pin bolts to 26 ft-lb.","Reinstall wheel. Torque lugs to 130 ft-lb in a star pattern.","**Critical:** Pump the brake pedal 5\u201310 times before driving to seat the pads against the rotor. The first pedal will go to the floor if you skip this. Verify firm pedal before moving the vehicle.","Re-torque lugs after 50\u2013100 miles."],"pros":["Standard floating caliper design","Common Akebono / Centric parts at any parts store","90 minutes per axle once you've done it","No special tools beyond a C-clamp"],"cons":["Easy to forget the pedal-pump step (and rear-end someone)","Slide pin neglect = premature pad wear","Larger Rubicon rotors not interchangeable with Sport","Lug torque is high (130 ft-lb) \u2014 needs a real torque wrench, not a breaker bar guess"]},{"id":"jl-brake-rotor","title":"Brake Rotor Replacement","vehicle":"jl","category":"brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":140,"high":380},"tools_required":["13mm socket","18mm socket","21mm socket","torque wrench","C-clamp","wire brush","brake cleaner","jack stands","micrometer"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wheel removal","rotor measurement"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2026"],"tags":["brakes","rotors","safety-critical","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM front rotor \u2014 standard brakes (0.95\" thick)","part_number":"68273502AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Mopar OEM front rotor \u2014 heavy duty brakes (1.1\" thick)","part_number":"68250085AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"Mopar OEM rear rotor (328mm solid)","part_number":"68564973AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Powerstop Z36 rotor + pad kit (front and rear)","vendor":"Powerstop","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"JL Brake Rotor Sizes and Codes Reference","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/brake-rotor-sizes-codes.57269/"},{"title":"Mopar 68273502AB Brake Rotor Specifications","url":"https://store.mopar.com/oem-parts/mopar-brake-rotor-68273502ab"}],"alternatives":["jl-brake-pads"],"summary":"JL Wranglers ship with one of three front rotor packages \u2014 verify which one you have before ordering. Standard rotors are 0.95\" thick (PN 68273502AB), heavy-duty are 1.1\" thick (PN 68250085AB). Wrong rotor won't fit. Full job runs 2 hours, $140 OEM front pair or $320 for a full four-corner kit.","description":"The JL Wrangler shipped from the factory with three distinct front brake configurations: BR2/BR3/BR6 packages with 1.1\" thick front rotors, and BRY with 0.945\" rotors. Standard Sport and Sahara trims got the thinner BRY setup; Rubicon and tow-package trucks got the heavier 1.1\" rotors. The bracket and caliper bolt to the same knuckle, but the rotor itself is taller \u2014 install the wrong rotor and the pads won't reach.\n\nHow to tell which you have without VIN decoding: pop a wheel and measure with a micrometer. Anything 27mm or thicker (new) is heavy-duty. 24mm range is standard. The Mopar build sheet identifies the brake package by code (BR2, BR3, BR6, BRY) but most owners don't have it handy. Measuring is faster than digging up the build sheet.\n\nRotor wear vs warp: like the JK, the JL front rotors don't tolerate creek crossings well. A glowing rotor dunked in cold water sets the casting slightly out-of-round and you'll feel it as steering-wheel shimmy under braking from highway speeds. Resurfacing rarely lasts more than a single pad cycle. Replace the rotor when warp shows up.\n\nOEM Mopar rotors are mid-grade castings \u2014 perfectly adequate for stock use. Powerstop Z36 and EBC Premium upgrade rotors cost the same to a little more and run cooler, which matters for lifted JLs running 35s or for tow rigs. Drilled-and-slotted \"performance\" rotors offer no real benefit on a sub-5,000-lb vehicle with single-piston calipers \u2014 the drill holes become crack initiators after a few hundred heat cycles.\n\nThe procedure mirrors the JK closely but with two differences. First, JL caliper slider bolt torque is slightly higher (13\u201315 ft-lb depending on year), still way lower than the bracket bolts. Second, the JL master cylinder is more sensitive to backflow when you compress the piston \u2014 definitely open the reservoir cap and have a rag ready.","steps":["Crack lug nuts (19mm), lift the front, support on stands at the frame rails.","Remove front wheels. Note brake package: measure rotor thickness with micrometer to confirm.","Open the brake fluid reservoir cap. Drape a rag.","Remove the two 13mm caliper slider bolts on the back of the caliper. Slide the caliper off and support with a hanger \u2014 never let it hang from the brake hose.","Compress the caliper piston back into the caliper bore using a C-clamp and an old pad. Move slowly.","Pull old pads from bracket. Note shim orientation.","Remove the two 18mm caliper bracket bolts at the knuckle. Lift bracket off. Bag it; it's reusable.","Pull the old rotor off the hub. Use M8x1.25 puller-hole bolts if stuck.","Wire-brush the hub face clean. Rust ridges cause runout that returns as pedal pulsing.","Install new rotor (correct thickness for your brake package). Light anti-seize on the hub mating face only \u2014 never on the friction surface.","Reinstall bracket and torque bracket bolts to 110 ft-lb.","Install new pads if pad life is below 5mm. Apply light brake grease to pad backs and slider pins.","Slide caliper over the pads. Reinstall slider bolts and torque to 14 ft-lb.","Reinstall wheels, torque lugs to 110 ft-lb.","Pump brake pedal 6\u20138 times until firm. Top off fluid.","Bed in pads: 8\u201310 moderate stops from 35 mph, then 2\u20133 hard stops from 50 mph. Cool down without dragging."],"pros":["OEM Mopar rotors well-priced and widely stocked","Same caliper architecture as JK \u2014 common tools, similar procedure","Powerstop Z36 upgrade is bolt-on, cost-effective for lifted rigs","Job under 2 hours on a familiar truck"],"cons":["Three different rotor packages from the factory \u2014 wrong part won't fit","Drilled-and-slotted rotors offer zero benefit and crack faster","Master cylinder sensitive to piston backflow \u2014 must open reservoir cap","Rotors warp early on rigs that see water crossings"]},{"id":"jl-8hp75-trans","title":"8HP75 Automatic \u2014 Service Intervals and Fluid","vehicle":"jl","category":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":220,"high":400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","torx bits","torque wrench","fluid pump","scan tool with trans temp read or Tazer"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","fluid level by temperature procedure"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["transmission","fluid","maintenance","8HP"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar ZF 8 & 9 Speed ATF (1 quart)","part_number":"68218925AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"ZF Lifeguard 8 ATF (1 liter, OEM-equivalent)","vendor":"ZF","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Mopar 8HP75 filter and pan gasket kit","part_number":"68225344AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Blau pre-packaged 8HP transmission service kit","vendor":"Blauparts","price_estimate_usd":380}],"citations":[{"title":"JL 8HP Specific Service Details (JL Wrangler Forums)","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/jl-specific-auto-transmission-service-details.48248/"},{"title":"Blauparts 8HP50/8HP75 ATF Change Kit","url":"https://www.blauparts.com/jeep-wrangler-transmission-fluid-atf-change-kit-8-speed-850re-8hp50.html"},{"title":"ZF8 50K DIY Procedure (JL Wrangler Forums)","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/zf8-50k-mile-transmission-service-diy-how-to-instructions.94302/page-17"}],"alternatives":["jl-transfer-case-jl","jl-etorque-understanding"],"summary":"Jeep markets the 8HP75 as \"lifetime fill,\" but ZF \u2014 the company that designs it \u2014 specifies service every 50,000-80,000 miles, and the gearbox lasts dramatically longer when you actually do it.","description":"The 8HP75 (Mopar code 850RE) is the same ZF-designed eight-speed used in BMW, Ram, and Maserati. It's a remarkable piece of engineering, but the \"lifetime fluid\" claim is a marketing convenience. ZF's own service literature calls for fluid changes at 50,000-80,000 miles, and shops that rebuild these boxes see the same failure pattern: burnt fluid leads to mechatronic and clutch-pack wear by 120k-150k miles. A $250 fluid service saves a $6,000 rebuild.\n\nThe fluid is non-negotiable: Mopar 68218925AB (ZF 8 & 9 speed ATF) or genuine ZF Lifeguard 8. Do not use a \"compatible\" or \"multi-vehicle\" ATF. The friction modifiers are specific to the wet clutch packs, and the wrong fluid causes shift flare and clutch glazing within thousands of miles.\n\nThe JL pan is metal with a separate bolt-on filter \u2014 meaning you can reuse the pan but should replace the filter and gasket every service. Capacity is roughly 9.5 quarts total, but a drain-and-fill only swaps about 5-5.5 quarts (the torque converter holds the rest). For best results, drain-fill twice with 1000 miles between, or run a fluid exchange machine.\n\nThe hard part isn't draining \u2014 it's filling to the correct level. There is no dipstick. The fill plug doubles as a level check, and the procedure requires the fluid at 86\u00b0F-122\u00b0F (30\u00b0C-50\u00b0C), the vehicle level on a lift or all four jack stands, and the trans in Park with the engine running. You need a scan tool (or Tazer) that reads transmission fluid temperature. Below 86\u00b0F you under-fill; above 122\u00b0F you over-fill. Both kill the box.\n\nIf you've never done this and don't have a scan tool that reads trans temp, this is the maintenance task to pay a dealer or independent shop to do. The fluid is cheap; the access is straightforward; the temperature window is what trips people up.","steps":["Drive the Jeep until trans is warm (10-15 minutes), then let it cool. Goal is 86\u00b0F-122\u00b0F at fill time.","Lift all four wheels to level the vehicle. Trans pan rail must be horizontal.","Place a wide drain pan under the trans. Remove the level/fill plug first (8mm Allen) \u2014 if you can't get this off, stop, because you can't refill.","Remove pan bolts in a crisscross pattern. Drop the pan slowly \u2014 it will be full of fluid.","Remove the bolt-on filter. Note its orientation. Install new filter and new pan gasket. Torque pan bolts to 89 in-lb in crisscross pattern.","From the fill port, pump in fresh fluid until it begins to dribble out (about 5-5.5 quarts).","Start the engine, foot on brake, cycle through P-R-N-D-2-1 holding each gear 3 seconds, return to Park.","With engine running, monitor trans fluid temp via scan tool or Tazer. When temp hits 86\u00b0F-122\u00b0F, fluid should be at a steady dribble from the fill port. If running fast, pump in more; if dry, you're under-filled.","Install fill plug, torque to 26 ft-lb.","Drive 10 miles checking for leaks and shift quality. Plan a second drain-and-fill at 1,000 miles to refresh the fluid that stayed in the converter."],"pros":["Extends transmission life by tens of thousands of miles","Pan and filter are accessible without dropping the trans","Mopar fluid is widely available"],"cons":["Requires a scan tool that reads trans fluid temperature","Temperature window for fill is narrow","Two drain-and-fills (or a flush machine) needed to fully refresh fluid","Wrong ATF causes shift flare within thousands of miles"]},{"id":"jl-etorque-understanding","title":"eTorque Mild Hybrid \u2014 What It Is and What It Isn't","vehicle":"jl","category":"drivetrain","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["OBD-II scanner (optional)"],"skills_required":["reading vehicle documentation"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["hybrid","electrical","battery","warranty"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"What is eTorque and does my Jeep Wrangler have it?","url":"https://metalcloak.com/blog/what-is-e-torque"},{"title":"48 volt eTorque battery reliability discussion","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/48-volt-etorque-battery-reliability-system.74361/"},{"title":"Battery issues and How to test 48V eTorque Battery","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/battery-issues-and-how-to-test-48v-etorque-battery.111007/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"eTorque is a mild hybrid system that replaces the alternator with a 48V belt-driven motor generator, smooths start-stop, and adds about 90 lb-ft (2.0L turbo) or 130 lb-ft (3.6L V6) of brief assist torque. It is not a plug-in hybrid and it does not let you drive on electric power alone.","description":"If you bought a 2019 or newer JL with the 2.0L turbo, you have eTorque whether you wanted it or not \u2014 it's standard on that engine. On the 3.6L Pentastar, eTorque was optional and is identified by the \"eTorque\" badging or by the second battery under the rear seat. The system has three pieces: a 48V lithium battery pack (under the rear passenger seat), a belt-driven motor generator unit (MGU) where the alternator used to live, and a DC-DC converter that steps 48V down to charge the conventional 12V starter battery.\n\nHere is the part most people miss: the 12V battery still cranks the engine on a cold start, exactly like a non-hybrid Jeep. The MGU only takes over to restart the engine after an auto stop-start shutoff. So when you turn the key first thing in the morning, you're using the same lead-acid battery any JK owner has been using for fifteen years. The 48V battery handles the smooth restart at red lights, captures regen energy on coastdown, and feeds the MGU when it pushes 90 to 130 lb-ft into the crankshaft for the first second or two of acceleration. That brief torque assist is what makes the 2.0L feel quicker than its 270 hp on paper.\n\nCommon eTorque problems on JL forums cluster around three things. First, the 12V starter battery still dies \u2014 owners often blame eTorque but the 12V is a normal AGM battery that ages on a normal AGM battery timeline (4\u20136 years in Phoenix heat). Second, the 48V battery occasionally throws codes for hybrid battery coolant sensor or temp sensor faults; dealerships generally replace under warranty. Third, some early 2.0L trucks reported weekly drain even with 14.4V charging \u2014 almost always traced to a parasitic draw, not the hybrid system. The 48V battery and related components are covered by an 8 year / 80,000 mile federal hybrid warranty, so most of the expensive failures are not coming out of your wallet.\n\nWhat eTorque does not do: it does not give you all-electric range, it does not let you bypass the 12V system if it dies, and it does not produce enough sustained torque to matter once you're above idle. Treat it as a smarter alternator that also softens the auto stop-start hand-off.","steps":["Identify whether your JL has eTorque by looking for the badge on the rear quarter panel or the second battery box under the rear passenger seat.","If you see a low-battery warning, test the 12V starter battery first \u2014 same drill as any AGM battery.","If the 48V hybrid system throws a code (P1A0A, P0A7D, P0AFA, others), document it and take it to a dealer while still under 8/80k warranty.","Do not disconnect or service the orange-shielded 48V cables \u2014 high-voltage hybrid wiring requires shop-level safety procedures.","Maintain the 12V battery normally: clean terminals, check resting voltage at 12.6V, replace at 4\u20136 years."],"pros":["48V battery, MGU, and related components covered 8 years / 80,000 miles","Smooths the auto stop-start restart","Adds brief torque assist on takeoff","Improves city MPG by 1\u20132 mpg"],"cons":["12V starter battery still dies on a normal schedule, confusing owners","Hybrid components are dealer-service items if out of warranty","Adds weight and complexity vs. a non-eTorque Pentastar","Trail repairs of the high-voltage side are not a DIY job"]},{"id":"jl-front-driveshaft-jl","title":"Front Driveshaft Replacement","vehicle":"jl","category":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":750},"tools_required":["15mm socket","18mm socket","torque wrench","marker","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","driveline geometry"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2026"],"tags":["driveshaft","rzeppa","cv-joint","vibration","lift"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM front driveshaft (JL non-Rubicon)","part_number":"68272525AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Mopar OEM Rzeppa CV joint repair kit","part_number":"68272534AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Adams Driveshaft 1310 front (heavy-duty)","vendor":"Adams Driveshaft","price_estimate_usd":595},{"name":"Tom Wood's JL front 1310 double cardan","vendor":"Tom Wood's","price_estimate_usd":575}],"citations":[{"title":"JK/JL front driveshaft CV failure \u2014 JK-Forum","url":"https://www.jk-forum.com/forums/stock-jk-tech-12/front-driveshaft-cv-replacement-245861/"},{"title":"Tom Wood's JL 1310 front shaft","url":"https://4xshaft.com/products/jk-jeep"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"A clunk on throttle tip-in or a vibration that starts after a lift is almost always the front Rzeppa CV joint \u2014 the stock shaft is the weak point on every JL, and lifting more than 2.5 inches will kill it faster.","description":"The JL front driveshaft uses a Rzeppa CV joint at the transfer case end and a single u-joint at the axle pinion. The Rzeppa design lets the shaft articulate without the harmonic vibration you get from a u-joint at a steep angle. Stock-height JLs run them for the life of the truck. Lifted JLs eat them, and the failure mode is consistent: a torn boot, grease slung across the underbody, and a clunk every time torque transfers through the joint.\n\nThe failure progression is predictable. The Rzeppa boot tears or weeps \u2014 sometimes from age, more often from articulation past the joint's design limit. Grease escapes. Dirt and water enter. The bearings inside the joint dry out and start to score. You hear a clunk on acceleration or deceleration, then a vibration that worsens as speed climbs. Catch it at the torn-boot stage and you can clean, regrease, and reboot the joint with a Mopar repair kit around $180. Wait until the clunk shows up and the bearings are already damaged \u2014 replace the joint or the whole shaft.\n\nThree replacement paths exist. The OEM Mopar shaft (68272525AC for non-Rubicon JL) at roughly $450 is a direct fit, but it is the same Rzeppa design that just failed. If the Jeep is stock and stays stock, OEM is fine. If you lifted it, replace the failure with something better. A heavy-duty 1310 shaft from Adams Driveshaft or Tom Wood's at $575\u2013$595 uses a double-cardan front joint, which tolerates the steeper operating angle a lifted JL imposes. Heavier-walled tubing means it survives rock hits and torque from larger tires.\n\nDiagnose before you order. Drop the front shaft, hold the CV end, and try to articulate it through its range. A healthy joint moves smoothly and silently. A failed joint clicks, binds, or rolls roughly. Inspect the boot for tears and the centering yoke for play \u2014 non-greasable yokes (211544X) and greasable yokes (211355X) both wear. Check the rear u-joint at the pinion end while the shaft is down; replacing one joint without inspecting the other is leaving a job half done.","steps":["Park on level ground, chock the rear wheels, and raise the front. Support on jack stands.","Mark the front shaft flange and the pinion yoke with a paint pen so you can reinstall in the same orientation. Phasing matters.","Remove the four 15mm bolts holding the shaft to the front axle pinion yoke.","Remove the four bolts at the transfer case flange (typically 15mm).","Lower the shaft, supporting the weight \u2014 it is not heavy but the CV end is fragile if dropped on concrete.","Inspect both joints. If only the CV is bad and the rest of the shaft is sound, install a Mopar repair kit per its instructions. Otherwise install the new shaft.","Bolt the new shaft up with the alignment marks aligned. Torque flange bolts to 22 ft-lbs at the pinion and 22 ft-lbs at the transfer case. Verify clearance from exhaust and skids before lowering the Jeep."],"pros":["Eliminates the clunk and vibration immediately","Repair kit option keeps the shaft if the bearings are still good","Heavy-duty aftermarket shafts handle lift, 35s, and trail use","Job is 1\u20132 hours with hand tools"],"cons":["OEM shaft repeats the same failure mode under lift","Aftermarket heavy-duty shafts run $575+","Boots tear from articulation, not just age \u2014 lifted Jeeps see more frequent failures","Pinion seal often weeps after a hard CV failure and may need replacement too"]},{"id":"jl-air-filter-jl","title":"Engine Air Filter Replacement \u2014 3.6L","vehicle":"jl","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":0.5},"cost_usd":{"low":18,"high":35},"tools_required":["flathead screwdriver","hands"],"skills_required":["none"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2025"],"tags":["fluids-filters","maintenance","preventive"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM Air Filter (3.6L Pentastar)","part_number":"68257030AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"K&N Drop-In Performance Filter","part_number":"33-5079","vendor":"K&N","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Mopar Performance High-Flow Filter","part_number":"P5160245AA","vendor":"Mopar Performance","price_estimate_usd":65}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar 68257030AA Air Filter \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/mopar/air-filter-3.6l-jeep-wrangler-jl-68257030aa"},{"title":"Jeep Air Filter Change Wrangler JL 3.6L \u2014 CheaperJeeperTV","url":"https://cheaperjeepertv.com/jeep-air-filter-change/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Pop four clips, swap the filter, done. The JL 3.6L Pentastar uses Mopar 68257030AA \u2014 replace it every 30,000 miles or sooner if you run dusty trails.","description":"The JL's airbox sits at the front passenger corner of the engine bay with four metal spring clips holding the lid down. No tools required for a stock replacement \u2014 though a flathead screwdriver helps pry the clips if they've never been opened. The whole job runs about 10 minutes and the OEM Mopar filter is $20\u201325 from any dealer or Quadratec. Aftermarket drop-ins from K&N or aFe are double the price; the genuine Mopar paper element flows plenty for a stock 3.6L and traps finer dust than oiled cotton-gauze filters.\n\nOpen the hood and look at the front passenger side. The airbox is the big black plastic shroud with the intake snorkel running up to the cowl. Run a finger along the seam between the lid and the lower box \u2014 you'll feel the four spring clips, two front and two rear. Pop each clip outward with your thumb or a screwdriver tip. Lift the lid up and toward the front of the Jeep; the intake tube on the back stays connected, the lid just hinges.\n\nThe old filter pulls straight up. Note its orientation \u2014 the rubber seal sits on top, the pleats face down. Wipe out any leaves or dirt in the lower airbox with a shop rag before dropping the new filter in. Don't blow it out with compressed air unless you've removed the MAF sensor tube \u2014 debris can fire backward through the system and contaminate the mass airflow sensor.\n\nDrop the new filter in with the seal facing up and pleats facing down. Press the lid back down \u2014 make sure the front edge tucks under its lip \u2014 and snap the four clips back into place. Pull on the lid once to confirm it's seated. If you feel air whistle through the seal when the engine runs, the lid isn't fully clipped.\n\nMost JL owners running pavement-only can stretch this to 40,000 miles. Phoenix dust, Moab trips, or sand washes drop the interval to 15,000\u201320,000. Hold the old filter up to a shop light; if you can't see light through it, it's overdue.","steps":["Open the hood and locate the airbox at the front passenger corner.","Pop the four spring clips on the airbox lid (thumb or flathead screwdriver).","Lift the lid up and toward the front; the intake tube stays connected.","Lift the old filter straight up out of the box.","Wipe out any debris in the lower airbox with a rag \u2014 do not use compressed air.","Set the new filter in with seal facing up, pleats facing down.","Tuck the front edge of the lid under its lip and press the lid down.","Snap all four spring clips back into place.","Tug the lid to confirm it's seated, close the hood."],"pros":["10-minute job with no tools required","OEM Mopar filter is $20\u201325 and fits perfectly","No risk of over-oiling and contaminating the MAF (a known issue with some oiled cotton filters)"],"cons":["Performance gains from aftermarket filters are mostly marketing on a stock 3.6L","Spring clips can pinch fingers if they snap shut on you","Hot-rodded JLs running tunes may still want a full intake system, not just a drop-in"]},{"id":"jl-battery-replacement","title":"Battery Replacement \u2014 Start-Stop and eTorque Systems","vehicle":"jl","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"electrical","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":450},"tools_required":["10mm socket","13mm socket","ratchet","battery terminal brush","memory saver (optional)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2025"],"tags":["electrical","battery","start-stop","etorque"],"parts":[{"name":"Main AGM H7 battery (94R, 80Ah, 850 CCA)","part_number":"MTX-94R / H7","vendor":"Interstate / Mopar","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Mopar OEM eTorque main battery","part_number":"56029720AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Auxiliary AGM battery (group 400, 200 CCA)","part_number":"AUX14 / AUX14-1","vendor":"DieHard / Super Start","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Odyssey AGM94R H7","part_number":"AGM94R","vendor":"Odyssey","price_estimate_usd":340}],"citations":[{"title":"JL Wrangler Forums \u2014 Main and Aux Battery Size","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/main-battery-aux-battery-size.123643/"},{"title":"2021 3.6 eTorque OEM Battery Specs","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/2021-3-6-etorque-oem-battery-specs.122345/"},{"title":"Wrangler Forum \u2014 Auxiliary Battery AGM Discussion","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/auxiliary-battery-is-it-an-agm-battery.2442093/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The JL uses an AGM H7 main battery \u2014 replace it with AGM, not a flooded lead-acid, or the start-stop system will fail and the BCM will log faults.","description":"The 2018+ Wrangler JL ships with an AGM H7 (BCI group 94R) main battery \u2014 typically 80 Ah and 850 CCA. eTorque trims (2.0L turbo and 3.6L with the 48V mild hybrid) add a small 12V AUX14 auxiliary AGM battery rated around 200 CCA, plus the separate 48V lithium-ion pack that handles the mild hybrid functions. The 48V pack carries an 8-year warranty and is not a DIY part. The auxiliary 12V AGM and the main H7, however, are both owner-serviceable.\n\nUse AGM only. The JL's body control module is configured for AGM charging voltage and current profiles. Drop in a flooded lead-acid battery and the BCM will overcharge it on highway runs and undercharge it during ESS stop events, which kills the battery in months and throws \"Service Stop/Start System\" warnings. Quality H7 AGM options include Mopar OEM (Interstate MTX-94R/H7), Odyssey AGM94R, Optima YELLOWTOP DH7, and the OEM eTorque part 56029720AA. Expect to pay $250\u2013$350 for a quality H7 AGM.\n\nThe auxiliary battery on start-stop and eTorque trims lives on the passenger side firewall under a small cover. It powers electronics during the ESS engine-off pause so cabin power, infotainment, and HVAC don't drop out. When it fails, ESS stops working and you'll see a service message \u2014 but the truck still runs fine on the main battery alone. Replacement AUX14 batteries run $90\u2013$140.\n\nAfter replacing either battery, the BCM needs to register the new battery so it can manage charging correctly. On non-eTorque JLs the system usually self-relearns within a few drive cycles. On eTorque trims, a Mopar dealer or aftermarket scan tool (Topdon, Autel, wiTECH) is recommended to perform a battery registration. Without registration, the alternator may not properly charge the new battery, shortening its life by half.","steps":["Park on level ground and turn off all accessories. Open the driver door so the BCM stays awake briefly.","Optional: plug a memory saver into the OBD-II port to preserve radio presets and adaptive transmission learning.","Open the hood. Loosen the 10mm negative (black) terminal clamp on the main battery first. Tuck the cable aside.","Loosen the 10mm positive (red) terminal clamp and remove the plastic cover. Tuck the cable aside.","Remove the 13mm battery hold-down bracket bolt at the front base of the battery.","Lift the battery straight up \u2014 it weighs roughly 50 pounds.","Clean the tray and terminals with a battery brush. Inspect for corrosion or moisture damage.","Set the new AGM battery in place with terminals matching the original orientation.","Reinstall the hold-down bracket and torque the 13mm bolt snug \u2014 do not overtighten.","Connect the positive terminal first, then the negative. Tighten the 10mm clamps until snug.","For eTorque or start-stop trims, perform a battery registration with a scan tool that supports BCM coding.","For the auxiliary battery: open the passenger-side firewall cover, disconnect the small terminals (10mm), remove the strap, and swap. Reverse to install."],"pros":["30\u201360 minute job in the driveway","$250 DIY vs. $500+ at the dealer","Restores start-stop function and clears service messages","AGM batteries handle deep-cycle abuse better than flooded"],"cons":["Battery registration may require a scan tool on eTorque trims","AGM costs roughly 2x a flooded H7","The 48V lithium pack on eTorque is dealer-only","AUX14 auxiliary battery is buried behind a firewall cover"]},{"id":"jl-differential-fluid-jl","title":"Differential Fluid Change \u2014 Dana 44 Front and Rear","vehicle":"jl","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":45,"high":90},"tools_required":["3/8 ratchet","13mm socket","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench","RTV gasket maker"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024","2025-2026"],"tags":["axles","gear-oil","service-interval","trac-lok"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar 75W-85 GL-5 gear oil (1 quart)","part_number":"68218057AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"Mopar Trac-Lok friction modifier (4 oz)","part_number":"04318060AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Mopar differential cover gasket / RTV","part_number":"68003557AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Permatex Ultra Black RTV","part_number":"82180","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"Motobilt \u2014 Jeep Wrangler Differential Fluid Capacity Chart","url":"https://motobilt.com/blogs/tech/jeep-wrangler-differential-fluid-capacity-chart"},{"title":"JL Wrangler Forums \u2014 Rubicon Rear Differential Gear Oil Capacity Surprise","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/rubicon-rear-differential-gear-oil-capacity-surprise.16811/"},{"title":"JL Wrangler Forums \u2014 Differential Fluid Discussion","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/diff-fluid-wrangler-jl.97387/page-3"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The JL Dana 44 axles take less fluid than the JK they replaced \u2014 about 1.5 quarts per axle \u2014 and Mopar specifies 75W-85 GL-5 gear oil, with 4 oz of friction modifier added to any Trac-Lok differential.","description":"The JL inherited the Dana 44 designation but the axles themselves are new \u2014 they're called AdvanTEK and they hold noticeably less fluid than the JK Dana 44. Plan on 1.4 to 1.6 quarts per axle (front and rear), not the 2.3 quarts you'd put in a JK. Overfilling is a real risk because the fill plug is positioned higher than the actual fill line on some axle covers \u2014 fluid coming out of the hole doesn't always mean the diff is full to spec. Trust the manual capacity and measure what goes in.\n\nMopar's recommended fluid is 75W-85 GL-5 hypoid gear oil (part #68218057AA). The owner's manual for some JL configurations also lists 75W-140 for severe trailer-towing use. For 99% of owners running street and trail, 75W-85 is correct. Any name-brand GL-5 in that viscosity works \u2014 Mobil 1 Synthetic 75W-90 is a common and acceptable substitute, despite being a half-step heavier.\n\nTrac-Lok limited-slip rear axles (standard on Sahara, optional on Sport S) require friction modifier. The Mopar additive is part #04318060AC, 4 ounces per axle. Without it, the clutches will chatter on slow corners \u2014 you'll hear it as a series of clunks from the rear end when turning. Lockers (Rubicon front and rear) do not need friction modifier.\n\nThe first fluid change matters most. Factory fill carries break-in metal from the ring and pinion. Mopar's service interval is 67,500 miles for normal use, 30,000 for severe service \u2014 but pulling the cover at 5,000\u20137,500 miles after delivery and replacing the fluid is cheap insurance. After the first change, every 30,000 miles is reasonable; sooner if you submerge the axles or wheel in mud often.\n\nThere is no drain plug on the AdvanTEK covers \u2014 you remove the cover to drain. That makes this a bigger job than it sounds, because you'll need to clean the cover, the magnet, and the housing mating surface, then re-seal with RTV. Bolts are 13mm, torque to 30 ft-lb in a cross pattern. Wait at least an hour for the RTV to skin before adding fluid.","steps":["Park on level ground. Warm the axles by driving a few miles \u2014 warm fluid drains cleaner.","Place a wide drain pan under the diff cover. Remove the fill plug FIRST (1/2\" ratchet drive). If it won't break loose, stop \u2014 you don't want to drain a diff you can't refill.","Crack the bottom cover bolts, then loosen the upper bolts. Leave the top two snug and let the cover hang open at the bottom to drain.","Once drained, remove all bolts and pull the cover. Inspect the magnet \u2014 fine fuzz is normal, chunks or chips are not.","Clean the cover, magnet, and housing mating surface with brake cleaner. Both surfaces must be bare metal and dry.","Apply a 1/8\" bead of Ultra Black RTV around the cover, encircling each bolt hole. Install cover and torque bolts to 30 ft-lb in a cross pattern.","Wait one hour for RTV to set.","Use a fluid pump to add 1.5 quarts of 75W-85 GL-5 through the fill hole. Add 4 oz friction modifier first if Trac-Lok.","Reinstall fill plug, torque to 25 ft-lb. Clean any drips."],"pros":["Cheap insurance against ring-and-pinion wear","Catches early bearing failure (metal on magnet)","30,000-mile interval is reasonable for a trail-driven JL","Lockers don't need friction modifier \u2014 simpler than older Trac-Lok-only platforms"],"cons":["No drain plug \u2014 full cover removal required","RTV cure time blocks the job for an hour","Fill plug position can fool you into overfilling \u2014 measure what goes in","Trac-Lok chatter is the symptom of missed friction modifier, and the fix is another fluid change"]},{"id":"jl-oil-change-jl","title":"Oil Change \u2014 Pentastar 3.6L and eTorque","vehicle":"jl","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"engine_oil","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":50,"high":95},"tools_required":["13mm socket","oil filter cap socket (76mm, 14-flute)","drain pan (6+ quart)","torque wrench","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-present"],"tags":["oil-change","pentastar","3.6l","etorque","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"0W-20 full synthetic oil (5\u20136 quarts depending on year)","part_number":"MS-12633","vendor":"Mopar / API SP rated equivalent","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"Mopar oil filter (cartridge)","part_number":"68191349AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Drain plug crush washer","part_number":"6104787AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":1}],"citations":[{"title":"2018+ JL Wrangler owner's manual \u2014 3.6L oil spec 0W-20","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/care/manuals.html"},{"title":"JL 3.6L Pentastar oil capacity (5 qt non-eTorque, 5.5 qt eTorque)","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com"},{"title":"Pentastar cartridge filter housing torque 25 Nm","url":"https://www.moparrepairconnect.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The JL Pentastar moved from 5W-20 (JK) to 0W-20 full synthetic. Use a cartridge filter \u2014 there's no spin-on. Drain plug torques to 20 ft-lb, filter cap to 18 ft-lb. eTorque models hold half a quart more oil than non-eTorque.","description":"The 3.6L Pentastar in the JL is the same basic engine that was in the late JK, but FCA changed the oil spec from 5W-20 to 0W-20 for fuel economy. Either viscosity will run the engine; the 0W-20 reduces cold-start friction and helps the start-stop and eTorque systems behave smoothly. Use what the manual calls for and the OBD-II long-term fuel trims will stay where they should.\n\n**Capacity.** 5.0 quarts on non-eTorque (2018+) and 5.5 quarts on eTorque mild-hybrid models with filter change. Always verify with the dipstick \u2014 overfilling causes the same intake-valve-coking issue the Pentastar is already prone to from direct injection.\n\n**Filter.** Cartridge type, same plastic housing on top of the engine as the JK. Mopar 68191349AC is the OEM part. The plastic cap cracks under abuse \u2014 a 76mm 14-flute cap socket is the only correct tool. If a previous shop used channel locks and the cap is gouged, replace it before it splits in service (Mopar 68191344AB, ~$30).\n\n**eTorque caveat.** eTorque adds a 48V belt-driven starter/generator (BSG) on the front of the engine. It doesn't change the oil change procedure, but it does mean the engine may auto-start during the change if you don't disconnect the 48V battery or follow the service mode procedure (turn ignition to RUN, then to OFF, then immediately disconnect 12V negative). Don't skip this on eTorque trucks.","steps":["Run engine 2\u20133 minutes to thin the oil. Shut off.","**eTorque only:** Cycle key to RUN then OFF, then immediately disconnect 12V negative terminal. This prevents auto-start during the change.","Pull the engine cover (friction fit).","Loosen the filter cap one turn to let oil drain into the housing before pulling the drain plug.","Position drain pan under oil pan. Remove 13mm drain plug. Drain 5+ minutes.","Remove filter cap completely. Lift out old cartridge. Wipe housing.","Replace cap O-ring. Lubricate new O-ring with fresh oil. Snap new cartridge into cap. Reinstall, torque to 25 Nm (18 ft-lb).","Install drain plug with new crush washer. Torque to 20 ft-lb.","Add 5 qt (non-eTorque) or 5.5 qt (eTorque) of 0W-20 full synthetic through the cap.","Reconnect 12V if disconnected. Start engine; verify oil pressure light goes out within 3 seconds.","Wait 5 minutes, check dipstick. Top off as needed \u2014 do not overfill."],"pros":["Top-mount filter, no crawling","30\u201360 minute job","Common 0W-20 synthetic at any parts store","Same procedure regardless of door count or transmission"],"cons":["Plastic filter cap requires care","eTorque adds a battery-disconnect step","Wrong viscosity (especially old 5W-30 inventory) will trigger fuel trim drift on long-term monitors","Pentastar still loves to coke intake valves \u2014 frequent oil changes help but don't solve it"]},{"id":"jl-spark-plugs-jl","title":"Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 3.6L Pentastar","vehicle":"jl","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":50,"high":90},"tools_required":["3/8\" ratchet","6-inch extension","5/8\" spark plug socket","torque wrench (in-lb capable)","T20 Torx"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2026"],"tags":["ignition","preventative"],"parts":[{"name":"NGK Laser Iridium ILKAR7L11 (set of 6)","part_number":"94124","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Mopar OEM spark plug (set of 6)","part_number":"SP149875AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Dielectric grease (small tube)","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":4}],"citations":[{"title":"iFixit \u2014 Spark plug replacement, 2018-2024 JL 3.6L","url":"https://www.ifixit.com/Guide/How+To+Replace+the+Spark+Plugs+In+Your+2018%E2%80%932024+Jeep+Wrangler+JL+(3.6L)/185982"},{"title":"WAYALIFE JL torque specs reference","url":"https://www.wayalife.com/threads/torque-specs-jl-wrangler-reference-guide.52799/"},{"title":"JLwranglerforums \u2014 spark plug change on 3.6 Pentastar","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/spark-plug-change-on-3-6-penstar.122868/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Replace all six iridium plugs every 100,000 miles. Torque to 13 ft-lb (17.5 N\u00b7m) \u2014 the 12mm Pentastar plugs strip if you over-tighten. Plan on an hour in the driveway.","description":"The 3.6L Pentastar in the JL uses 12mm spark plugs \u2014 smaller than the 14mm plugs in older Jeep engines. That matters for two reasons. First, the spark plug socket is 5/8\" (16mm), not the 13/16\" that fits the 4.0L. Second, the thinner shell strips its threads at much lower torque, so a regular click-type torque wrench set to \"feel\" is not good enough. Use a wrench that reads in inch-pounds or one that goes down to single-digit foot-pounds.\n\nNGK ILKAR7L11 is the direct factory equivalent and runs about $55 for a set of six. Mopar's branded plug is the same NGK part with a Chrysler box and a markup. There is no benefit to going up a heat range or to a one-step-colder plug on a stock motor \u2014 the OEM iridium plugs are correct for stock and lightly modified Pentastars. Skip the anti-seize; NGK explicitly says not to use it on iridium plugs, and the plated threads do not need it.\n\nAccess on the JL is straightforward. The coil packs sit on top of each cylinder, held by a single 10mm bolt. Remove the bolt, twist the coil to break the rubber boot seal, lift it off, and you are looking at the plug well. Use a 6-inch extension and the 5/8\" plug socket. The plug well is deep \u2014 a magnetic or rubber-insert socket keeps the old plug from dropping into the head when you back it out.\n\nReinstall each new plug by hand first to confirm thread engagement \u2014 never start a plug with a ratchet. Torque to 13 ft-lb (17.5 N\u00b7m). A dab of dielectric grease inside the coil boot helps it pull off cleanly the next time. Reinstall the coil with the 10mm bolt, repeat for the remaining five cylinders. Total job time is about an hour the first time, 30 minutes after that.\n\nSymptoms that mean it is time: rough idle at startup, misfire code (P0301\u2013P0306), or a noticeable loss of low-end response. Most JLs make it to the 100,000-mile interval cleanly. Track-driven or extreme cold-climate Jeeps benefit from inspecting at 75,000.","steps":["Cold engine. Disconnect the negative battery cable (10mm).","Remove the engine cover (pulls straight up off four rubber grommets).","Pick a coil pack. Remove the 10mm coil hold-down bolt.","Unclip the coil electrical connector. Twist and lift the coil out.","Drop the 5/8\" plug socket on a 6-inch extension into the well. Back the plug out counterclockwise.","Verify the gap on the new plug (factory pre-gap is correct \u2014 do not regap iridium).","Hand-thread the new plug. Then torque to 13 ft-lb.","Apply dielectric grease inside the coil boot. Reinstall coil, bolt, and connector.","Repeat for the remaining five cylinders.","Reinstall engine cover, reconnect battery, start, listen for smooth idle."],"pros":["100,000-mile interval \u2014 set-and-forget maintenance","Job runs about $60 in parts vs. $200+ at a shop","Restores throttle response and cold-start smoothness","Direct access \u2014 no manifold removal needed"],"cons":["12mm plugs strip easily if you over-torque","Coil boots can tear if the rubber has aged","Plug socket must be the deep 5/8\" type with a retainer","Skipping torque spec is the #1 way to crack a coil"]},{"id":"jl-transfer-case-fluid","title":"Transfer Case Fluid Change \u2014 MPT/FCA 8HP Considerations","vehicle":"jl","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":70},"tools_required":["3/8 ratchet","13mm hex bit (fill)","13mm hex bit or square drive (drain)","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fluid handling"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2025"],"tags":["transfer-case","fluids","mp3022","atf+4"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar ATF+4 Automatic Transmission Fluid (quart)","part_number":"68218057AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Valvoline MaxLife ATF (multi-vehicle, MS-9602 compatible)","vendor":"AutoZone","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Fluid transfer pump (quart bottle adapter)","vendor":"Amazon","price_estimate_usd":15}],"citations":[{"title":"Trail Wrangler \u2014 Jeep JL Transfer Case fluid change","url":"https://trailwrangler.com/2021/05/13/jeep-jl-transfer-case-fluid-change-guide/"},{"title":"JL Wrangler Forums \u2014 MP3022C fluid change","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/transfer-case-mp3022c-fluid-change.58861/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Use Mopar ATF+4 (or an MS-9602-rated multi-vehicle ATF) in every JL transfer case \u2014 MP3022 on Rubicon, NV241 on Sport/Sahara, MP1522 on 4xe \u2014 and budget 1.7 to 2.0 quarts for the refill.","description":"The JL's transfer case requires ATF, not gear oil \u2014 this trips up owners coming from XJ or TJ platforms where the t-case ran the same 75W-90 as the axles. The Mopar spec is MS-9602, which is the same fluid family as ATF+4. Any name-brand ATF+4 or a multi-vehicle ATF that lists MS-9602 compatibility (Valvoline MaxLife, Castrol Transmax) will hold up. Mopar's official position is ATF+4 only. The aftermarket position is that MS-9602-compatible fluids work fine; pick your comfort level.\n\nCapacity depends on the case. The MP3022 (Rubicon two-speed with 4LO) shows around 1.7\u20131.85 quarts on a proper drain and refill. The NV241 on Sport/Sahara is similar. The MP1522 found on 4xe takes about 2.0 quarts. Service manuals occasionally underreport \u2014 buy two quarts and bring a third if you have it.\n\nBoth plugs use a 3/8\" square drive or a 13mm hex on most years \u2014 pop them open before you crawl under to verify. Open the fill plug first; same rule as a diff \u2014 if you can't open the fill, don't drain. Position a low-profile pan under the case, open the drain plug, let it run. Inspect what comes out. ATF should look reddish; if it's brown or smells burnt, the case has been working hard and you may want to plan a second drain in 5,000 miles to flush remaining contamination.\n\nReinstall the drain plug at 20 ft-lbs. Pump new ATF into the fill hole until it overflows \u2014 that's the level, do not try to add more. Reinstall the fill plug at 20 ft-lbs. Run the rig through the 2H/4H/N/4L positions a few times in a parking lot to circulate the new fluid through the chain.","steps":["Drive the rig 10 minutes to warm the fluid.","Park level, set the parking brake, chock front tires.","Locate the transfer case behind the transmission, between the front and rear driveshafts.","Loosen the fill plug first to verify it opens.","Place a drain pan, open the drain plug, let drain fully.","Inspect drained fluid color and smell.","Reinstall drain plug at 20 ft-lbs with fresh crush washer if equipped.","Pump fresh ATF+4 (or MS-9602 ATF) into the fill hole until it overflows.","Reinstall the fill plug at 20 ft-lbs.","Cycle through 2H/4H/4L positions to circulate."],"pros":["One of the cheapest scheduled-service items on a JL","Catches water intrusion that ruins chain and bearings","1 hour with the rig on the ground (no lift required)","Fluid is the same as transmission and power steering \u2014 easy to stock"],"cons":["Mopar restricts the official spec to ATF+4, limiting bulk purchase options","Service position is awkward without ramps","Two stripped plug heads (square-drive 3/8) are common \u2014 chase down a fresh tool if yours is rounded"]},{"id":"jl-alignment-jl","title":"Alignment After Lift \u2014 JL-Specific Specs","vehicle":"jl","category":"steering","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":100,"high":180},"tools_required":["tape measure","alignment shop or string-line setup","tie rod wrench"],"skills_required":["measurement","tape-measure toe check"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2023","2024-2026"],"tags":["caster","toe","death-wobble-prevention","lift-aftercare"],"parts":[{"name":"Synergy adjustable front lower control arms (pair)","part_number":"8062","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":540},{"name":"Rough Country adjustable lower arms","part_number":"11700","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"Teraflex adjustable front lower arms","part_number":"1815110","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":470}],"citations":[{"title":"JL Wrangler Alignment Specs Discussion","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/jl-wrangler-wheel-alignment-specs-toe-caster-camber.17832/"},{"title":"Basic DIY JL Front End Alignment \u2014 WAYALIFE","url":"https://www.wayalife.com/threads/write-up-basic-diy-jeep-jl-wrangler-front-end-alignment.56571/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"A 2\"+ lift on a JL drops caster below the 4\u00b0 safety floor; you need adjustable lower control arms to bring it back to 4.5\u00b0\u20135.5\u00b0 or the front end will wander at highway speed.","description":"The JL runs more positive caster from the factory than the JK did \u2014 Sport models target 5.35\u00b0, Sahara 5.05\u00b0, Rubicon 4.80\u00b0, with toe at 0.20\u00b0 \u00b1 0.15\u00b0 total and camber at -0.25\u00b0. That extra caster is what makes the JL feel less twitchy than the JK at highway speed. The problem: lift kits rotate the axle pinion up, and on a solid-axle Jeep that means caster goes down by roughly 1\u00b0 per inch of lift. Two inches of lift and you're at 3\u00b0\u20134\u00b0 caster, below the Jeep service manual's 4.0\u00b0 minimum. That's when the wandering and floaty steering complaints start.\n\nWhat can and can't be adjusted: toe is the only spec you can change with stock parts \u2014 loosen the tie rod adjuster sleeve clamps, rotate the sleeve, retighten. Camber on a solid axle is fixed by the axle housing; there is no adjustment. Caster requires changing the angle of the axle, which means adjustable lower control arms (cheapest path) or adjustable upper and lower arms (best path for big lifts). The lowers reposition the axle fore-aft; the uppers rotate the pinion. With just lowers you can claw back about 2\u00b0\u20133\u00b0 of caster. With both, you can fully restore stock geometry.\n\nTarget settings for a lifted JL: caster 4.5\u00b0\u20135.5\u00b0, total toe 0.15\u00b0\u20130.20\u00b0 toe-in, camber whatever the axle gives (don't worry about it). Higher caster up to 6\u00b0 feels tighter but eats tires faster and adds steering effort. Below 4\u00b0 you risk wander and death-wobble vulnerability.\n\nThe tape-measure toe check works at home: roll the Jeep forward 6 feet to settle the suspension, measure between the front of the tires at hub height, then between the rear of the tires at the same height. The front number should be 1/16\" to 1/8\" smaller than the rear. Adjust the tie rod sleeve to dial it in. A proper alignment shop will charge $100\u2013$140 to do this with a rack and verify caster, which is worth doing after any lift.","steps":["Confirm the lift is fully installed, all control arm bolts torqued at ride height.","Drive the Jeep 20\u201350 miles to settle bushings.","Bring to alignment shop with a printout of target specs (caster 4.5\u00b0\u20135.5\u00b0, total toe 0.15\u00b0\u20130.20\u00b0 toe-in).","If caster is below 4\u00b0, do not road-test at highway speed \u2014 install adjustable lower control arms first.","With adjustable lowers, lengthen them to push axle rearward, increasing caster.","With adjustable uppers, shorten them to rotate pinion up.","Have shop re-measure after each adjustment.","Set total toe to 0.15\"\u20130.20\" toe-in.","Center the steering wheel by adjusting equal turns on each tie rod end.","Test drive \u2014 straight steering, no pull, no wander at 65 mph."],"pros":["Restores correct caster after a lift","Eliminates highway wander","Reduces death wobble risk by keeping caster above 4\u00b0","Adjustable arms also re-center axle fore-aft"],"cons":["Caster correction requires aftermarket arms","Camber is non-adjustable \u2014 you live with what the axle gives you","Big lifts (4\"+) need both upper and lower adjustable arms","Setting caster too high eats tires and adds steering effort"]},{"id":"jl-death-wobble-jl","title":"Death Wobble on JL \u2014 Different from JK but Same Root Causes","vehicle":"jl","category":"steering","subcategory":"diagnostics","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":1800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","pry bar","torque wrench","metric sockets"],"skills_required":["front-end inspection","recall lookup","torque to spec"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2023","2024-2024"],"tags":["death-wobble","safety-critical","track-bar","recall","TSB","ball-joints"],"parts":[{"name":"OEM JL front track bar (post-recall)","part_number":"68398018AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":245},{"name":"Synergy JL HD adjustable track bar","part_number":"8861-15","vendor":"Synergy Mfg","price_estimate_usd":419},{"name":"Steel steering gear box (TSB 08-074-20)","part_number":"68250506AE","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":980},{"name":"Dynatrac HD ball joints JL (pair)","part_number":"DA60-2X1052-J","vendor":"Dynatrac","price_estimate_usd":480}],"citations":[{"title":"NHTSA Recall 18V-675 \u2014 JL track bar bracket weld","url":"https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/rcl/2018/RCRIT-18V675-4839.pdf"},{"title":"JLWranglerForums \u2014 Track Bar Bracket Weld Recall","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/track-bar-bracket-weld-issue-recall-announced/"},{"title":"4xeForums \u2014 Lower ball joint TSB 02-004-21 build dates 9/16/20\u20138/4/21","url":"https://www.4xeforums.com/threads/if-your-jeep-wrangler-jl-was-built-between-9-16-20-and-8-4-21-your-lower-ball-joints-may-loose-causing-death-wobble.3631/"}],"alternatives":["jl-track-bar-jl","jl-ball-joints-jl"],"summary":"Death wobble on a JL has the same root cause as the JK \u2014 loose or worn front-end components triggered by a bump \u2014 but the JL adds three platform-specific suspects: the recalled 2018 track bar bracket weld (NHTSA 18V-675), the aluminum steering gear box (TSB 08-074-20), and the lower ball joint TSB on trucks built between September 2020 and August 2021. Check those three first, then inspect the rest of the front end like a JK.","description":"The JL inherited the JK's solid front axle with a linked drag-link-and-tie-rod setup. Same geometry, same failure mode. What changed is what Jeep tried to do to fix the JK's reputation \u2014 and some of those changes created new failure points.\n\n**The 2018 track bar bracket recall.** Early JLs (built before December 2018) had a frame-side track bar bracket where the weld was placed off the seam. Under load the bracket separated from the frame, and the axle could shift freely. NHTSA recall 18V-675 covers the fix. If your JL is a 2018 build, run the VIN through NHTSA's recall lookup before doing anything else \u2014 if the recall has not been performed, the dealer fixes it free.\n\n**The aluminum steering gear box.** 2018 JLs left the factory with an aluminum steering gear box that flexed under high steering input. TSB 08-074-20 replaces it with a steel unit. Symptom is vague on-center steering and wobble that returns after every alignment. Dealer handles this under warranty for affected VINs.\n\n**Lower ball joints, builds 9/16/20 to 8/4/21.** TSB 02-004-21-REV A covers lower front ball joints that loosen prematurely and trigger death wobble. If your JL was built in that window, the dealer inspects and replaces them free of charge.\n\n**Then it's a JK inspection.** With recalls and TSBs cleared, run the same systematic front-end inspection you would on a JK. Get the wheels off the ground, rock each tire 12/6 and 9/3 for ball joint and tie rod end play, watch every joint while a helper turns the steering wheel, and check track bar bolt torque (110 ft-lb axle / 125 ft-lb frame on JL). Re-torque after any wheeling trip. A stabilizer cannot cure death wobble on a JL any more than it can on a JK \u2014 fix the root cause.\n\n**A note on aftermarket fixes.** Some owners install a dual-pin track bar bracket (Synergy, Metalcloak) when the frame-side bracket has wallowed even after a recall fix. Adjustable track bars from Synergy and RockJock allow re-centering the axle after a lift, which matters because off-center geometry amplifies wobble. Heavy-duty tie rod kits (RockJock Currectlync JL CE-9701JL, Steer Smarts Yeti XD) replace the factory bent rod with a straight DOM tube that resists impact-bend on rocks.","steps":["Run your VIN through NHTSA's recall lookup at nhtsa.gov/recalls. Address recall 18V-675 (track bar bracket) if it shows open.","Ask the dealer to check TSB 08-074-20 (steering gear box) and TSB 02-004-21 (ball joints) against your build date. Both are dealer-paid if eligible.","With recalls cleared, put the front wheels on jack stands. Rock each tire at 12/6 and 9/3 \u2014 diagnose worn ball joints vs. tie rod ends.","Have a helper turn the wheel slowly while you watch each joint. Independent slop is a worn part.","Verify track bar bolt torque: 110 ft-lb at the axle, 125 ft-lb at the frame.","Inspect track bar bushings and bracket. Pry-test the bar at the axle bracket for any movement.","Replace worn components, re-torque, alignment shop afterward.","Road test gradually toward the speed wobble occurred at, with bump tests in between."],"pros":["Recalls and TSBs are free at the dealer","Solves the wobble at the source rather than masking it","Catches related wear before it becomes a roadside breakdown"],"cons":["Diagnosis still takes a couple of hours","Aluminum gear box replacement is dealer-only on out-of-warranty trucks ($900+)","Lifted JLs with 35\"+ tires need geometry correction in addition to part replacement","Some owners report repeat wobble even after full front-end replacement on early 2018 trucks \u2014 track bar bracket reinforcement may be required"]},{"id":"jl-tie-rods-jl","title":"Tie Rod and Drag Link Replacement","vehicle":"jl","category":"steering","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":500,"high":1200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","tie rod separator","metric sockets","torque wrench","tape measure","grease gun"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2026"],"tags":["steering","death-wobble","recall"],"parts":[{"name":"Apex Chassis 2.5 Ton ProLock Tie Rod & Drag Link Kit (Steel)","part_number":"KIT115","vendor":"Apex Chassis","price_estimate_usd":900},{"name":"Synergy Heavy Duty Chromoly Tie Rod (JL)","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":525},{"name":"Steer Smarts YETI XD JL Drag Link","vendor":"Steer Smarts","price_estimate_usd":750}],"citations":[{"title":"Apex Chassis KIT115 JL Steering Kit","url":"https://apexchassis.com/products/kit115-jeep-jl-steering-kit-steel"},{"title":"JL Wrangler Tie Rod & Drag Link Guide","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/guide-to-replacing-stubborn-steering-components-tie-rod-and-drag-link/"}],"alternatives":["jl-alignment-jl","jl-steering-stabilizer-jl"],"summary":"The JL's factory steering was the subject of a 2019 service campaign and is still the weakest link on lifted trucks \u2014 a heavy-duty tie rod and matching drag link tighten the front end and stop the wandering most owners complain about.","description":"Jeep updated the JL tie rod under service bulletin W30 because the original two-piece design developed play that drove an epidemic of loose, vague steering. Even the updated factory tie rod is fine for stock trucks on stock tires, but it's the first thing to fail once you add 35\"+ tires, a lift, or any meaningful trail use. The drag link uses the same forged-end design and tends to develop the same play a few thousand miles later.\n\nThree configurations cover most builds. A complete steel kit like Apex Chassis KIT115 ($900) gets you a 2.5-ton tie rod and drag link with greasable forged ends in one box \u2014 that's the simplest upgrade if you want everything done in a single afternoon. Synergy and Steer Smarts both sell standalone chromoly tie rods around $500\u2013$550, which is the right call if your drag link is still tight and only the tie rod has play. For the loaded overland or rockcrawler build, Steer Smarts YETI XD covers both pieces with 30 mm ball joints at the top of the price range.\n\nAluminum tie rods are sold for the JL but corrosion at the sleeve threads and stripped jam nuts have shown up enough times in the forums that most experienced JL builders steer clear unless weight is the absolute priority.\n\nTorque specs matter on this truck. The knuckle nuts are 47 ft-lb, the pitman arm nut is 77 ft-lb, and the steering stabilizer bolt/nut are 50/55 ft-lb. Jam nuts on 3/4-16 sleeves start at 180 ft-lb \u2014 don't guess them.","steps":["Park level, chock rear wheels, loosen front lug nuts, jack the front axle and put it on stands.","Remove both front wheels for clearance.","Measure the tie rod and drag link center-to-center lengths and write them down.","Remove the steering stabilizer to give yourself room to swing.","Pop the cotter pins, remove the knuckle nuts (tie rod) and pitman arm nut (drag link), and separate with a proper tie rod separator. Avoid pickle forks here \u2014 they wreck the boots if you plan to keep any factory pieces as backups.","Pre-assemble the new tie rod and drag link to match the recorded lengths.","Install the tie rod first. Torque knuckle nuts to 47 ft-lb, then continue tightening to align cotter pin slots. Install new cotter pins.","Install the drag link. Torque the pitman arm nut to 77 ft-lb and the knuckle end to 47 ft-lb.","Tighten all sleeve clamps or jam nuts to the manufacturer's spec (180 ft-lb minimum for 3/4-16). Grease zerks.","Reinstall the steering stabilizer (50 ft-lb bolt, 55 ft-lb nut), wheels (95 ft-lb lugs), and get an alignment."],"pros":["Cures the post-W30 wandering on lifted trucks","Greasable rebuildable ends","Works with the factory steering damper"],"cons":["Best kits run nearly $1,000","Alignment required","Aluminum bargain options can corrode and strip"]},{"id":"jl-coil-replacement","title":"JL Coil Spring Replacement","vehicle":"jl","category":"suspension","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets (13mm, 15mm, 17mm, 18mm, 21mm)","torque wrench","pry bar","spring compressor (if going taller)"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","suspension disassembly"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2023","2024-2026"],"tags":["lift","coils","ride-quality","geometry"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM front coil 2-door Sport","part_number":"68250203AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Teraflex 2.5\" coil set (front + rear)","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Rough Country 2.5\" coil-only kit JL","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"Metalcloak 3.5\" Game-Changer coils JL","vendor":"Metalcloak","price_estimate_usd":540}],"citations":[{"title":"TORQUE SPECS \u2014 JL Wrangler Reference Guide","url":"https://www.wayalife.com/threads/torque-specs-jl-wrangler-reference-guide.52799/"},{"title":"Jeep JL Wrangler Torque Specs \u2014 Metalcloak","url":"https://metalcloak.com/blog/jeep-jl-wrangler-torque-specs"},{"title":"Complete Torque Values for Jeep JL Wrangler","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/complete-torque-values-for-jeep-jl-wrangler/"}],"alternatives":["jl-shock-replacement","jl-lift-options","jl-control-arms"],"summary":"JL coil swaps are bolt-in if you drop the axle far enough to clear the coils \u2014 the catch is torquing control arm and track bar bolts at ride height, not full droop, or you'll preload the bushings and chew through them.","description":"The JL inherited the JK's coil-sprung solid-axle layout but tightened the geometry \u2014 control arms are stiffer, bushings are different, and the front track bar mounts higher on the frame. The coil swap procedure itself is among the most approachable suspension jobs on the JL because nothing is captured: drop the axle, pull the spring out the top, swap, reassemble. No spring compressor needed for OEM-height or 2.5\" coils.\n\nThe mistake people make on JLs is final-torquing the control arm and track bar bolts with the axle drooping on jack stands. Polyurethane and rubber bushings establish their neutral position based on where the suspension was sitting when the bolt got torqued. Tighten them at full droop, and they're pre-loaded against the resting suspension position the entire time the Jeep is on the ground. The result is harsh ride, premature bushing wear, and rubber bushings that crack within 20K miles. The fix is to either set the axle at ride height on a drive-on lift or to set everything to snug, lower the Jeep, then crawl underneath with a torque wrench.\n\nCoil selection on the JL splits into three lanes. Stock-height replacement (Mopar OEM, $80\u2013$120 per spring) is for owners whose original coils sagged or who lost one to corrosion. 2.5\" leveling coils (Rough Country, Teraflex, Mopar 2\" lift kit) get you to 35\" tires with no other geometry tricks. 3.5\" coils (Metalcloak Game-Changer, Rock Krawler) start to need extended brake lines, adjustable track bar, and adjustable control arms to keep the front end pointed straight. Don't run 3.5\" coils with stock arms and the stock track bar \u2014 bump steer and pulling-to-one-side will follow.\n\nJL coils are progressive-wound on most aftermarket sets. The bottom coils are heavier so they support the rig's curb weight; the top coils are lighter so they compress earlier under small bumps. This is why a Rubicon front spring will sit different heights on a Sport versus a 4xe \u2014 same spring, different weight on it. Weigh your front axle if you're swapping models. The 4xe carries about 200 lb more on the front axle than a gas Sport.\n\nThe bumpstop retainer cup bolt at the top of the coil pocket is hand-tight only; don't crank it. The spring retainer clip at the lower coil seat takes 16 ft-lb. Top shock bolts are 17 ft-lb with a small 1/4\" wrench on the shaft to keep it from spinning. Front lower shock bolt is 70 ft-lb (M14). Track bar at axle: 125 ft-lb. Track bar at frame: 55 ft-lb. Control arm bolts at axle and frame: 125 ft-lb, torqued at ride height.","steps":["Crack lug nuts (21mm) on the corner you're working. Lift the front of the Jeep, support on stands under the frame rails.","Remove front wheels. Support the axle under the differential with a floor jack.","Disconnect both front sway bar end links at the axle (15mm). On Rubicons with electronic disconnect, cycle the disconnect first, then unbolt.","Disconnect the lower shock bolts (18mm) at the axle.","Disconnect the track bar at the axle bracket (21mm, 125 ft-lb to reinstall).","Slowly lower the floor jack to drop the axle. The coils will lose tension as the axle drops 5\u20136 inches.","Remove the spring retainer clip (13mm bolt, 16 ft-lb to reinstall).","Pull the coil out the top with the bumpstop still in the bumpstop cup.","Install the new coil \u2014 make sure it seats in the upper pocket isolator and the lower spring perch.","Reinstall the retainer clip (16 ft-lb).","Jack the axle back up. Reconnect shock, track bar, and sway bar links \u2014 all to \"snug\" only.","Lower the Jeep to the ground, drive 20 feet to settle bushings.","Final-torque control arms (125 ft-lb), track bar (125 ft-lb at axle, 55 at frame), sway bar links (70 ft-lb) at ride height.","Get an alignment. Caster spec: 4.5\u00b0\u20136.0\u00b0."],"pros":["No spring compressor needed for stock-height or 2.5\" coils","Bolt-in swap with common metric tools","Wide aftermarket options from $200 to $600","2.5\" coils clear 35s on a Rubicon with no other parts"],"cons":["Torquing at full droop wrecks bushings \u2014 easy mistake to make","4xe weights front axle differently than gas; coil choice matters","3.5\"+ lifts need adjustable track bar, adjustable control arms, longer brake lines","Always plan an alignment after the swap"]},{"id":"jl-lift-options-jl","title":"JL Lift Kit Options \u2014 What Changed from JK","vehicle":"jl","category":"suspension","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":600,"high":4000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","spring compressor","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["suspension work","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["lift","geometry-correction"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar 2\" Lift Kit with Fox shocks","vendor":"Mopar / Quadratec","price_estimate_usd":1599},{"name":"AEV 2.5\" Dualsport RT","vendor":"AEV","price_estimate_usd":1379},{"name":"Rock Krawler 3.5\" Mid-Arm Stage 2","vendor":"Rock Krawler","price_estimate_usd":3876},{"name":"Geometry correction brackets (JL)","vendor":"Teraflex / JKS / Synergy","price_estimate_usd":200}],"citations":[{"title":"Aftermarket JL Lift Kit suspension options master list","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/aftermarket-jl-lift-kit-suspension-options-master-list.3690/"},{"title":"The Ultimate Jeep Wrangler Lift Kit Comparison","url":"https://trailrecon.com/blogs/news/jeep-wrangler-lift-kit-comparison-aev-rock-krawler-jock-pro-teraflex"},{"title":"Mopar 2\" Lift Kit Fox Series for JLU","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/mopar/2-inch-lift-kit-fox-series-shocks-jeep-wrangler-jl-unlimited"}],"alternatives":["jl-coil-replacement-jl","jl-alignment-jl"],"summary":"The JL keeps the JK's coil-sprung five-link layout but uses different spring rates, a wider front track bar angle, and a more expensive geometry-correction price tag \u2014 budget about $200 extra over a JK build for the same lift height.","description":"The JL chassis carries forward the five-link front and rear coil suspension from the JK. So you can still pick from spacer lifts, spring lifts, mid-arm kits, and long-arm kits. What changed is the geometry-correction tax. Caster on the JL drops faster per inch of lift, and the OEM geometry-correction bracket from Mopar/aftermarket costs around $200 for a JL versus about $90 for the JK. Plan for that.\n\nSpacer lifts (1.25\"\u20132\") are bolt-on coil spacers. Cheap, fast, and they keep the OEM ride. Mopar's own 2\" kit with Fox shocks at $1,599 is a popular middle path because it's covered if you also lease the Jeep. AEV's 2.5\" Dualsport RT ($1,379) is the expedition-grade spring lift for owners running heavy bumpers and a roof rack. Rock Krawler's 3.5\" Mid-Arm Stage 2 ($3,876) goes furthest with adjustable lower arms, adjustable track bars, and corrected sway bar links \u2014 that's a 37\" tire build.\n\nThe 4xe complicates lift selection: the rear battery pack changes ride height, and lifts under 2\" usually keep the factory shocks; lifts at 2.5\"+ require longer shocks specced for 4xe rear hardware. Confirm any kit you buy lists 4xe compatibility on the box.\n\nSway bar links changed in 2024 \u2014 Jeep updated hardware specs, so a link from a 2018 kit may not fit a 2024 build correctly. Check year-specific fitment before ordering.\n\nTire fitment guide: 33\" tires fit a stock JL with no lift if you trim the front fender liner. 35s need 2\"\u20132.5\" of lift plus 17\"+ wheels with proper backspacing. 37s need 3.5\"+ of lift on a non-Rubicon, or a Dynatrac/AEV kit on a Rubicon designed for that height.","steps":["Decide tire size first \u2014 that drives lift height, not the other way around.","Pick a kit type: spacer (cheap), spring lift (OEM-style ride), or mid-arm/long-arm (best geometry).","Verify 4xe and model-year compatibility with the kit vendor.","Order geometry correction (brackets or adjustable arms) for any lift over 1.5\".","Pull stock springs, install new components per kit instructions.","Re-center axle with the front track bar, then set toe to 1/16\"\u20131/8\" toe-in.","Get a rack alignment \u2014 JL alignment specs are tighter than JK."],"pros":["Wide range of OEM-blessed and aftermarket options","4xe-specific kits available","Backed by stronger factory steering than late JK"],"cons":["Geometry correction costs more than equivalent JK parts","4xe lifts can void some factory warranty coverage","Sway bar link hardware varies by year \u2014 verify before buying"]},{"id":"jl-shock-replacement","title":"Shock Absorber Replacement","vehicle":"jl","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":900},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets (18mm, 21mm)","torque wrench","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","torque sequencing"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2026"],"tags":["shocks","ride-quality","lift"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein B8 5100 Front Shock (0-2\" lift, JL Unlimited)","part_number":"33-292625","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Bilstein B8 5100 Rear Shock (2-3\" lift, JL Unlimited)","part_number":"33-292632","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Bilstein B8 5100 Rear Shock (3-4.5\" lift, JL)","part_number":"33-292977","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Fox 2.0 Performance Series IFP Rear Shock (JL)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox","price_estimate_usd":175},{"name":"OEM Mopar shock (replacement)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":95}],"citations":[{"title":"Bilstein 5100 Front JL Unlimited 33-292625","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/bilstein/front-5100-series-monotube-shock-absorber-jeep-wrangler-jl-unlimited-33-292625"},{"title":"Bilstein 5100 Rear JL 2-3\" lift 33-292632","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/bilstein/rear-5100-series-monotube-shock-absorber-jeep-wrangler-jl-unlimited-2-3-inch-lift-33-292632"},{"title":"Fox 2.0 Performance Rear IFP Shock JL","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/fox-racing-shox/rear-20-performance-series-ifp-shock-jeep-wrangler-jl"}],"alternatives":["jl-coil-replacement","jl-lift-options"],"summary":"Replace JL shocks when they leak, fade on long trails, or no longer settle after a bump \u2014 match shock length to your current lift height, not your future plans.","description":"Stock JL shocks are tuned soft for on-road comfort. They fade quickly on washboard, body-control gets sloppy after 60,000 miles, and the rear shocks are usually the first to leak. If you've added a lift, the stock shocks are likely topped out before full droop \u2014 meaning you've got internal damage even if they aren't visibly leaking.\n\nThe decision tree is short. Stock height and want a better ride? Bilstein 5100 monotubes (33-292625 front, 33-316321 rear for 0-1.5\" lift, 33-292632 rear for 2-3\" lift) are bolt-in upgrades at around $110 per corner. Lifted 2-3\"? Same Bilstein 5100s in the lifted length. Lifted 3\"+? Step up to remote-reservoir shocks like Fox 2.0 Performance Series IFP, Bilstein 8112, or King 2.5 \u2014 your shocks will see more heat and travel.\n\nAvoid the temptation to put a 4\" lift shock on a stock vehicle. The shock will top out at full extension, which damages the internal valving and gives you a harsh ride at any extension.\n\nInstall is the simplest suspension job on the JL. Unlike spring or control arm work, shock replacement doesn't touch alignment geometry. One side at a time, no spring tension released. Stock JL shock mounting uses 18mm and 21mm fasteners. Mopar service spec for shock-to-axle bolt is 75 ft-lb, shock-to-frame upper bolt is 60 ft-lb \u2014 confirm against the included instructions for your specific shock as some aftermarket units use different hardware.\n\nPre-extend new shocks before installing them upright (most monotubes ship gas-charged at full extension, but pre-cycling them by hand helps seat the valving). Don't install a shock upside down \u2014 the IFP gas charge needs to be at the top for proper damping.","steps":["Park on level ground; chock the opposite wheel.","Lift one corner; place jack stand under the frame, leaving the axle hanging.","Remove the wheel.","Spray penetrating oil on both shock mounting bolts; let sit 5 minutes.","Unbolt the lower shock bolt (at the axle bracket).","Unbolt the upper shock bolt (at the frame mount).","Compress the old shock by hand and remove.","Cycle the new shock 3\u20135 times by hand to seat valving.","Install upper bolt first (loose), then lower bolt.","Torque to spec \u2014 typically 75 ft-lb lower, 60 ft-lb upper (verify with your shock's instructions).","Reinstall wheel; torque lug nuts to 130 ft-lb.","Repeat for remaining corners.","Drive 50 miles, then re-check shock bolt torque (rubber bushings settle)."],"pros":["Cheapest meaningful ride upgrade","No alignment needed after","Bolt-in, no spring compressor required","Bilstein 5100s last 60,000+ miles on daily-driver JLs"],"cons":["Wrong-length shocks for your lift cause internal damage","Stock shock mount bolts can seize after Phoenix heat cycles \u2014 penetrant first","Remote-reservoir shocks need clearance routing for the reservoir hose","Rear shocks on Unlimited 4-doors are tight to the gas tank \u2014 work from below"]}];